#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ce72ec-4d09-54cb-a578-eae101bc6938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1 + z}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173579} {:answer/id #uuid "09476af1-0770-5e5d-bdf3-c45ea904d853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 + 3z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173580} {:answer/id #uuid "26928822-4036-5504-a56d-6d9c52aa148e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1 - 3z}{2z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173581} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5bddd7-914d-5164-94a6-e3078675ee1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1 - 2z}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173582} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7c8ffb-553f-5771-93c7-567fdc154bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1 + z}{2z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173583}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f47fb17e-8686-5981-8687-3cc41d6ed00d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173578}] :question/id #uuid "489ad320-9026-5fb6-bac4-8d732b399bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-107802" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1 - 3x - 3y}{x + y}$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z = x + y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a6bddc-ab82-5ec6-afde-d00b334c9e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241264} {:answer/id #uuid "09f70782-96ea-53f6-8f9e-80c4729c6bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241268} {:answer/id #uuid "d579b6f2-cdce-5402-83f9-c79bb237818f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243069} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c9abf5-c7a7-5718-a3b6-86da7dc6fb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243119} {:answer/id #uuid "e537cb6a-f6c7-570c-adef-a9027acdab1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e537cb6a-f6c7-570c-adef-a9027acdab1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{z}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243217} :answer-field/id #uuid "10ce90bd-39f0-5d28-917d-33cd6143f545" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10d103bc-2f56-5346-9216-0cbfc3385289" :question/math-academy-id "q-108736" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}+6xy+3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3(x+y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3{z}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3{z}^{2}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175983 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31cb6b88-21af-58b2-9bbc-36b86d29f9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27x-9y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241619} {:answer/id #uuid "616d62d4-fd1d-56e0-aea1-01be6ea44e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242058} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c02940-6aae-590b-a846-f3b0124feef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-2y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242587} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fd5bee-c66f-5b57-832d-ffd1bffd7ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242869} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3e0d5a-55f1-5492-8d8f-c4889137f0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27x-9y-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc3e0d5a-55f1-5492-8d8f-c4889137f0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27x-9y-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243137} :answer-field/id #uuid "b05d6b4a-98d6-5cb1-8fde-f65da21468d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a92b889-cf95-57c9-b2d1-07f0f25b0f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-108857" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{9x-3y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=y+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3(3x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9z-2}{3z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\int (1+\\frac{2}{9z-2})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2|) & =x+K \\\\ z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2| & =3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =3x+C \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=27x-9y-2.\n$$"}]}
